members of Grace Presbyterian Church off  the I-15 in Charleston say Nevada is the  largest and most expensive public works  project has left them almost  inaccessible and struggling for  donations Ryan mathey joins us live to  explain their dilemma Ryan  yeah Kirsten Christian even we can  attest to some of these struggles we had  quite the time trying to find this  location and the big reason why this is  such a problem is this overpass right  over here it was built as part of the  neon project in 2018 now church members  say people cannot find a way inside it  means less money for good deeds and  their new location  so we lost the ability for a year to  worship and then when we came back  people couldn't find the property  nowadays Senior Pastor Jim Houston  hankin says these seats inside the Grace  Presbyterian Church sit mostly empty we  would average Maybe  10 12 visitors from the strip from out  of town who would come and join us for  worship on Sundays  that is completely dried up the reason  project neon he says the state's largest  and most expensive public works project  to date one billion dollars used to  enhance four miles of Interstate 15  Nevada's busiest stretch of highway you  can't even see the property even from  the freeway but the construction did not  enhance the church once five entrances  down to two both only one way with no  legal turnaround for roughly a mile from  either one once we had a funeral  here and the hearse could not find an  entrance to the church and showed up 30  minutes late  there's just nothing you can do to make  that right and with less visitors  willing to navigate the property less  donations during service that would go  to Ministries like feeding the hungry in  2018 the church sued the state saying  utilities were forced underneath Church  property against the landowner's will  that case now settled the church  receiving an undisclosed amount of money  to move to this new property on Durango  and Oquendo but it's still not enough  three to four million dollars short of  mirroring what they had here here so  it's difficult when we have to take  money that would have been used for  for Ministries like feeding the hungry  and so forth  now they're doing those fundraising  efforts on their website you can find  out more about it on our website and  regardless if those extra millions are  actually fundraised they plan to begin  constructing what they can afford this  spring reporting live from the Grace  Presbyterian Church Ryan Matthey 8 News  Now
 

